        {
            "content_html": "<img src=\"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/1761395?v=4\" width=\"64\" height=\"64\" alt=\"\"/><br/><h3 dir=\"auto\">Description</h3>\n<p dir=\"auto\">The <a href=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/color_value/color\" rel=\"nofollow\"><code class=\"notranslate\">color()</code> (<code class=\"notranslate\">color_value/color</code>)</a> is currently the top result for the query <a href=\"https://duckduckgo.com/?q=site%253Adeveloper.mozilla.org+color\" rel=\"nofollow\"><code class=\"notranslate\">color site:developer.mozilla.org</code> on DDG</a>, and I've observed there is no clear organic path to (intended) <a href=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/color\" rel=\"nofollow\"><code class=\"notranslate\">color</code> property (<code class=\"notranslate\">CSS/color</code>)</a> from there, what \"See also\" seems fit.</p>\n<hr>\n<h5 dir=\"auto\">Motivation</h5>\n<p dir=\"auto\">I am (ab)using DDG's \"bang\" («I'm feeling lucky»\") feature through a keyword search to get to MDN references as quickly as possible, and <code class=\"notranslate\">https://duckduckgo.com/?q=!%20site:developer.mozilla.org%20color</code> kicked me to a page I was not expecting, though, arguably, has more \"color\" keywords in the URL than any other page.</p>\n<h5 dir=\"auto\">Additional details</h5>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Also, I have the \"See also\" section pulled up before main content with userstyle, because quite often continuing elsewhere related is what I am coming for.  That's why I've hit addressed issue and why it knocked me off.</p>",
